[[:db/cas 17592186123680 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186123681] [:db/cas 17592186125180 :question/worked-solution nil "Set $y_2=u(x)\\cos x$ on an interval where $\\cos x\\ne0$. Differentiating gives\n$$\ny_2'=u'\\cos x-u\\sin x,\\qquad y_2''=u''\\cos x-2u'\\sin x-u\\cos x.\n$$\nSubstitution into $y''+y=0$ yields $u''\\cos x-2u'\\sin x=0$. Setting $w=u'$ gives $w'=2w\\tan x$, hence $w=C\\sec^2x$ and $u=C\\tan x+D$. Choosing $C=1,D=0$ gives $y_2=\\sin x$. This expression extends across the zeros of $\\cos x$ and satisfies the original equation everywhere. Since $W(\\cos x,\\sin x)=1$, the second solution is independent of the first."] [:db/cas 17592186335772 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\csc }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\csc }^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\left(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$" "Work first on an interval where the given solution $y_1(x)$ is nonzero. After simplifying $y_2$, verify it in the original equation to extend it across zeros of $y_1$ when the simplified function is defined there.\n\nAccording to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\csc }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\csc }^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\left(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124369 :question/problem "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\n- $u''=0$\n\n- $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\n- $u''-u=0$" "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\nI. $u''=0$\n\nII. $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\nIII. $u''-u=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186123686 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186123687] [:db/cas 17592186125181 :question/worked-solution nil "With $y_2=ux$, we have $y_2'=u'x+u$ and $y_2''=u''x+2u'$. Substitution gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2y_2''-xy_2'+y_2\n&=x^2(u''x+2u')-x(u'x+u)+ux\\\\\n&=x^3u''+x^2u'\\\\\n&=x^2(xu''+u').\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $x>0$, division by $x^2$ gives $xu''+u'=0$."] [:db/cas 17592186237945 :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{-3t}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-6t}+9u{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-6t}+9u{e}^{-3t})+2({u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t})-3u{e}^{-3t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}+9u+2{u}^{′}-6u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{e}^{-3t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{-3t}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}+9u{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-3t}-6{u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}+9u{e}^{-3t})+2({u}^{′}{e}^{-3t}-3u{e}^{-3t})-3u{e}^{-3t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-6{u}^{′}+9u+2{u}^{′}-6u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-3t}({u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228088 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186228089] [:db/cas 17592186229644 :question/worked-solution nil "To find the complementary solution, solve the associated homogeneous equation $y_c'+3y_c=0$. For $y_c\\ne0$, separating and integrating gives $\\ln|y_c|=-3x+C$, hence $y_c=Ae^{-3x}$. The zero solution is included by taking $A=0$, so $A$ may be any real constant."] [:db/cas 17592186305055 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-30.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x/5},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186305055 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x}-5.\n$$" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x/5}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 0+6\\beta =-30\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-6x/5}-5.\n$$\n\nCheck: $y_c'=-(6/5)Ae^{-6x/5}$, so $5y_c'+6y_c=0$. Also $y_p=-5$ gives $5y_p'+6y_p=-30$."] [:db/cas 17592186302514 :answer/value "A{e}^{-6x}-5" "A{e}^{-6x/5}-5"] [:db/cas 17592186229647 :question/worked-solution nil "The complementary solution is $y_c=Ae^{-3x}$. Since the forcing term is constant, try $y_p=\\beta$. Substituting into $y'+3y=6$ gives $3\\beta=6$, so $y_p=2$. Thus $y=Ae^{-3x}+2$. The initial condition gives $-2=A+2$, hence $A=-4$. Therefore\n$$\ny(x)=-4e^{-3x}+2.\n$$\nIndeed, $y'=12e^{-3x}$, so $y'+3y=6$ and $y(0)=-2$."] [:db/cas 17592186228106 :answer-field/correct nil "answer-quiz3-q332773"] [:db/cas 17592186151964 :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y - 3y = 0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n\n- $y(x) =- e^{x} + e^{3x}$" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{3x}$ are solutions to the equation $y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$\n\nII. $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$\n\nIII. $y(x) =- e^{x} + e^{3x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186151964 :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) =e^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = - e^{x} + e^{3x}$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "According to the superposition principle, if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c_1$ and $c_2$ are constants, then $y(x) = c_1 y_1(x) + c_2 y_2(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that $y_1(x) = e^{-x}$ and $y_2(x) =e^{3x}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(x) = c_1 e^{-x} + c_2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x) = 7e^{-x} - 6e^{3x}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, $c_1 = 7$ and $c_2 = -6.$\n\n- The function $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{3x}}{e^{-x}}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation. The superposition principle only applies to linear combinations of known solutions. It does not extend to the divisions of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x) = - e^{x} + e^{3x}$  is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the given equation because $e^x$ is not a solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."] [:db/cas 17592186150329 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186150332] [:db/cas 17592186152637 :question/worked-solution nil "The superposition principle applies because the equation is linear and homogeneous. Every function $c_1e^{-t}+c_2te^{-t}$ is a solution. Statement I uses $(c_1,c_2)=(0,-3)$, statement II uses $(1,-1)$, and statement III uses $(1/2,0)$. Thus all three statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186152637 :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(t) = e^{-t}$ and $y_{2}(t) = te^{-t}$ are solutions to the equation $y^{″} + 2y^{′} + y = 0$, which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(t) =-3te^{-t}$\n2. $y(t) = e^{-t} - te^{-t}$\n3. $y(t) = \\frac{e^{-t}}{2}$" "Given that $y_{1}(t) = e^{-t}$ and $y_{2}(t) = te^{-t}$ are solutions to the equation $y^{″} + 2y^{′} + y = 0$, which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(t) =-3te^{-t}$\nII. $y(t) = e^{-t} - te^{-t}$\nIII. $y(t) = \\frac{e^{-t}}{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186151996 :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n\n- $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{-2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^x$ are solutions to the equation $y''+y'-2y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(x) = \\dfrac{e^{-2x}}{e^{x}}$\n\nII. $y(x) = e^{-2x}\\cdot e^{x}$\n\nIII. $y(x) = e^{-2x} +e^{x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186151997 :question/problem "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n\n- $y(x) = -e^{2x}$" "Given that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 4y = 0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(x) = 2xe^{2x}$\n\nII. $y(x) = 3e^{2x}$\n\nIII. $y(x) = -e^{2x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186151367 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186151371] [:db/cas 17592186152810 :question/worked-solution nil "Every constant multiple $ce^{-x}$ is a solution by the superposition principle. Statements I and III use $c=2$ and $c=-9$, respectively. For statement II, let $y=xe^{-x}$. Then $y'=(1-x)e^{-x}$ and $y''=(x-2)e^{-x}$, so $y''-y=-2e^{-x}\\ne0$. Therefore the answer is I and III only."] [:db/cas 17592186152810 :question/problem "Given that $y_{1}(x) = e^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation $y^{″} - y = 0$, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n1. $y(x) = 2e^{-x}$\n2. $y(x) = xe^{-x}$\n3. $y(x) =-9e^{-x}$" "Given that $y_{1}(x) = e^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation $y^{″} - y = 0$, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nI. $y(x) = 2e^{-x}$\nII. $y(x) = xe^{-x}$\nIII. $y(x) =-9e^{-x}$"] [:db/cas 17592186152565 :question/problem "Given the set of functions $\\{x,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.167em}2x\\}$ defined on $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." "Given the set of functions $\\{x,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.167em}2x\\}$ defined on $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nFor all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186152566 :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${1, \\sin (x)}$ defined on $x ∈ R$, select the correct options in the following reasoning.Suppose for all $x ∈ R$, we have that\n$k_{1} \\cdot 1 + k_{2} \\cdot \\sin (x) = 0$,\nwhere $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf $x = 0$, we get {{field-1}}.\n\nIf $x = \\frac{π}{6}$, we get {{field-2}}." "Given the set of functions $\\{1, \\sin(x)\\}$ defined on $x ∈ R$, select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nSuppose for all $x ∈ R$, we have that\n$k_{1} \\cdot 1 + k_{2} \\cdot \\sin (x) = 0$,\nwhere $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\nIf $x = 0$, we get {{field-1}}.\n\nIf $x = \\frac{π}{6}$, we get {{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186308544 :question/problem "Given the set of functions $\\{\\hspace{0.167em}7\\operatorname{sin}x,\\hspace{0.167em}5\\operatorname{cos}x,\\hspace{0.167em}-5\\operatorname{cos}x\\}$ defined on $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." "Given the set of functions $\\{\\hspace{0.167em}7\\operatorname{sin}x,\\hspace{0.167em}5\\operatorname{cos}x,\\hspace{0.167em}-5\\operatorname{cos}x\\}$ defined on $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nFor all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186421312 :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nFor all $x\\in R,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." "Given the set of functions $\\{\\sin x,e^x,3\\sin x\\}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nFor all $x\\in R,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186421313 :question/problem "Given the set of functions ${{e}^{x},\\,x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nSuppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{k}_{2}\\cdot x=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${k}_{1}$ and ${k}_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=0,$ we get  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get  \n{{field-2}}.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is {{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." "Given the set of functions $\\{e^x,x\\}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nSuppose for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{k}_{2}\\cdot x=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${k}_{1}$ and ${k}_{2}$ are constants.\n\nFor example:\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=0,$ we get  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nIf $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we get  \n{{field-2}}.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1} &=0 \\\\ 2{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2){k}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is {{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}."] [:db/cas 17592186421310 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=-3x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=-3x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{y_1(x),y_2(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186151907 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\n- $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0.$\n\n- The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty).$" "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nII. $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0.$\n\n- The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty).$"] [:db/cas 17592186150317 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186150318] [:db/cas 17592186152635 :question/worked-solution nil "Both $e^{2x}$ and $e^{2x+3}=e^3e^{2x}$ satisfy $y''-y'-2y=0$. However, they are constant multiples of one another, so $Ae^{2x}+Be^{2x+3}=(A+Be^3)e^{2x}$ contains only one independent homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation $(r-2)(r+1)=0$ also gives $e^{-x}$, and the general solution is $C_1e^{2x}+C_2e^{-x}$. Therefore I and II are true, and III is false."] [:db/cas 17592186152635 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $y^{″} - y^{′} - 2y = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_{2}(x) = e^{2x + 3}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y = Ae^{2x} + Be^{2x + 3}$." "Consider the differential equation $y^{″} - y^{′} - 2y = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y_{1}(x) = e^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\nII. $y_{2}(x) = e^{2x + 3}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y = Ae^{2x} + Be^{2x + 3}$."] [:db/cas 17592186150365 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186150368] [:db/cas 17592186152643 :question/worked-solution nil "On an interval where $x\\ne0$, direct substitution shows that $x$ and $x^2$ both satisfy $x^2y''-2xy'+2y=0$. Their Wronskian is $x(2x)-x^2=x^2\\ne0$ on that interval. They form a fundamental set, so the general solution is $y=Ax+Bx^2$. All three statements are true."] [:db/cas 17592186152643 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} - 2xy^{′} + 2y = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = x$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_{2}(x) = x^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y = Ax + Bx^{2}$." "Consider the differential equation $x^{2}y^{″} - 2xy^{′} + 2y = 0$. Work on an interval where $x\\ne0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y_{1}(x) = x$ is a solution to the equation.\nII. $y_{2}(x) = x^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The general solution of the equation is $y = Ax + Bx^{2}$."] [:db/cas 17592186265795 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0.$ For $x>0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186452727 :answer-field/key "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation." "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186452728 :answer-field/key "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-t}$ is a solution to the equation." "${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-x}$ is a solution to the equation."] [:db/cas 17592186452729 :answer-field/key "The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}.$" "The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{1-x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186348312 :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(t)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t}+2(-{e}^{-t})+{e}^{-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{1-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{1-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-t}+2(-{e}^{1-t})+{e}^{1-t} & =(1-2+1){e}^{1-t}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-t}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{-t}{({e}^{1-t})}^{′}-{({e}^{-t})}^{′}{e}^{1-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(-{e}^{1-t})-(-{e}^{-t}){e}^{1-t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2t}+{e}^{1-2t} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0,$ the functions are not linearly independent, hence $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{1-t}.$” is false." "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(t)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+2(-{e}^{-x})+{e}^{-x} & =(1-2+1){e}^{-x}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{1-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x}+2(-{e}^{1-x})+{e}^{1-x} & =(1-2+1){e}^{1-x}=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{1-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & ={e}^{-x}{({e}^{1-x})}^{′}-{({e}^{-x})}^{′}{e}^{1-x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(-{e}^{1-x})-(-{e}^{-x}){e}^{1-x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2t}+{e}^{1-2t} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0,$ the functions are not linearly independent, hence $y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{1-x}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement “The general solution of the equation is $y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{1-x}.$” is false."] [:db/cas 17592186150347 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186150348] [:db/cas 17592186152640 :question/worked-solution nil "Set $y_2=u(x)\\cos5x$ on an interval where $\\cos5x\\ne0$. Differentiation and substitution give\n$$\ny_2''+25y_2=u''\\cos5x-10u'\\sin5x=0.\n$$\nLetting $w=u'$ yields $w'=10w\\tan5x$, so $w=C\\sec^2 5x$ and $u=\\frac C5\\tan5x+D$. Choosing $C=5,D=0$ gives $y_2=\\sin5x$, which extends across the zeros of $\\cos5x$ and solves the original equation on $\\mathbb R$. Since $W(\\cos5x,\\sin5x)=5\\ne0$, the general solution is\n$$\ny=A\\cos5x+B\\sin5x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186150353 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186150355] [:db/cas 17592186152641 :question/worked-solution nil "Set $y_2=u(t)e^{4t}$. Then\n$$\ny_2'=e^{4t}(u'+4u),\\qquad y_2''=e^{4t}(u''+8u'+16u).\n$$\nSubstitution into $y''-y'-12y=0$ gives $e^{4t}(u''+7u')=0$. Since $e^{4t}\\ne0$, let $w=u'$ and solve $w'+7w=0$: $w=Ce^{-7t}$ and $u=-\\frac C7e^{-7t}+D$. Taking $C=-7,D=0$ gives $y_2=e^{-3t}$. The Wronskian is $-7e^t\\ne0$, so\n$$\ny=Ae^{4t}+Be^{-3t}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186357490 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\left(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$" "Work first on an interval where the given solution $y_1(x)$ is nonzero. After simplifying $y_2$, verify it in the original equation to extend it across zeros of $y_1$ when the simplified function is defined there.\n\nAccording to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=6w\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ becomes \n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\left(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+{c}_{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=3\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186380277 :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=2w\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=1\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" "Work first on an interval where the given solution $y_1(x)$ is nonzero. After simplifying $y_2$, verify it in the original equation to extend it across zeros of $y_1$ when the simplified function is defined there.\n\nAccording to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-u\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =2{u}^{′}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=2w\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({c}_{1}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=1\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186150323 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186150327] [:db/cas 17592186152636 :question/worked-solution nil "Set $y_2=u(x)x$. We have $y_2'=u'x+u$ and $y_2''=u''x+2u'$, so substitution yields $x^2(xu''+u')=0$. Since $x>0$, set $w=u'$ and solve $xw'+w=0$. This gives $w=C/x$ and $u=C\\ln x+D$. Choosing $C=1,D=0$ yields $y_2=x\\ln x$. The Wronskian of $x$ and $x\\ln x$ is $x\\ne0$ on $(0,\\infty)$, so\n$$\ny=Ax+Bx\\ln x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186216661 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186216664] [:db/cas 17592186217799 :question/worked-solution nil "Because $x>0$, divide the equation by $2x$ to obtain\n$$\ny''+\\frac2x y'+\\frac12y=0.\n$$\nThus $P(x)=2/x$. Abel's identity gives\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x)=-\\frac2xW(x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217004 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\tan(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-\\tan(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ W & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=4$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ 4 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=4\\cos x.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217800 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{3\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{3} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(4) & =2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3\\cdot 4}=2{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{3\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{3} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(4) & =2{e}^{-3}{e}^{3\\cdot 4}=2{e}^{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217803 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{2}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}} \\\\ 6 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{2}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}} \\\\ 6 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317379 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 5 & =C \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5{e}^{-2\\cdot 3}=5{e}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 5 & =C \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5{e}^{-2\\cdot 3}=5{e}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317381 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-5W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-5W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-5\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-5x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-5x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-5\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-5} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5\\cdot 3}=2{e}^{-10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-5W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-5W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-5\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-5x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-5x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-5\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-5} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =2{e}^{5}{e}^{-5\\cdot 3}=2{e}^{-10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317382 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\left(\\frac{2}{x}+1\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317383 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{1} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{-1}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\pi ) & =5{e}^{-1}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}=5{e}^{-1}{e}^{-1}=5{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{1} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{-1}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\pi ) & =5{e}^{-1}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}=5{e}^{-1}{e}^{-1}=5{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186317384 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & ={x}^{3}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{{1}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =Ce \\\\ C & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)={e}^{-1}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & ={e}^{-1}{e}^{{2}^{3}}={e}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & ={x}^{3}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{{1}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =Ce \\\\ C & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)={e}^{-1}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & ={e}^{-1}{e}^{{2}^{3}}={e}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497358 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-6} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{6}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(6) & =5{e}^{6}{e}^{-3\\cdot 6}=5{e}^{-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-6} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=5{e}^{6}{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(6) & =5{e}^{6}{e}^{-3\\cdot 6}=5{e}^{-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186497417 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}({x}^{2}+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=10$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =\\frac{C}{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\ 10 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{10}{{2}^{2}+1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}({x}^{2}+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=10$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =\\frac{C}{{0}^{2}+1} \\\\ 10 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{10}{{2}^{2}+1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552402 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}(x+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{1+1} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{12}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{12}{3+1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}(x+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{1+1} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{12}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{12}{3+1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186552436 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(-1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(-1) & =C{e}^{-(-1)} \\\\ 6 & =C{e}^{1} \\\\ C & =6{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-2}=6{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(-1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(-1) & =C{e}^{-(-1)} \\\\ 6 & =C{e}^{1} \\\\ C & =6{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =6{e}^{-1}{e}^{-2}=6{e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186652897 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 2} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{8} \\\\ C & =3{e}^{-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=3{e}^{-8}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(5) & =3{e}^{-8}{e}^{4\\cdot 5}=3{e}^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 2} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{8} \\\\ C & =3{e}^{-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=3{e}^{-8}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(5) & =3{e}^{-8}{e}^{4\\cdot 5}=3{e}^{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186652936 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-xW(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-xW \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-{1}^{2}/2} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-1/2} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{1/2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =2{e}^{1/2}{e}^{-{3}^{2}/2}=2{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-xW(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-xW \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-{1}^{2}/2} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-1/2} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=2{e}^{1/2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =2{e}^{1/2}{e}^{-{3}^{2}/2}=2{e}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186217005 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -3 & =C{e}^{-8} \\\\ C & =-3{e}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSince $-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{ { {W ( x ) \\ne  0} \\text{ for all } {x \\in  \\mathbb{R}}}.}$\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{ {\\text{linearly independent on } {\\mathbb{R}}}.}$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\nW'(x)= \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{-4}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=-3$ to find $C{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -3 & =C{e}^{-8} \\\\ C & =-3{e}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=-3e^{8}e^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSince $-3e^{8}e^{-4x}\\neq 0$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we conclude that $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{ { {W ( x ) \\ne  0} \\text{ for all } {x \\in  \\mathbb{R}}}.}$\n\nSo, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{ {\\text{linearly independent on } {\\mathbb{R}}}.}$"] [:db/cas 17592186217801 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186217802 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 0} \\\\ 1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)={e}^{2x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317015 :answer-field/correct 17592186316190 17592186315279] [:db/cas 17592186317385 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(\\pi )=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\pi ) & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(\\pi )=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(\\pi ) & =C{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186317385 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(\\pi )=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(\\pi )=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(\\pi )=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(\\pi )=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186316650 :answer-field/correct 17592186315430 17592186315930] [:db/cas 17592186317386 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{3}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{3}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-3} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{{2}^{3}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{C}{8} \\\\ C & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{3}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{3}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{3}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-3} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{{2}^{3}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{C}{8} \\\\ C & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186365897 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}(x+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{1+1} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}(x+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{1+1} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186365912 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-3} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=2{e}^{3}{e}^{-3x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =C{e}^{-3} \\\\ C & =2{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=2{e}^{3}{e}^{-3x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186365912 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(1)=2.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(1)=2,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(1)=2.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(1)=2,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186385276 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)} \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)} \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186397617 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 0} \\\\ -1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=-{e}^{4x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=-1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 0} \\\\ -1 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=-{e}^{4x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186397632 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-2xW(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-2xW \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-2x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-{x}^{2}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-{2}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{-4} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-2xW(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-2xW \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-2x\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-{x}^{2}+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-{2}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{-4} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186397647 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0} \\\\ 0 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-3W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-3W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-3\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-3x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0} \\\\ 0 & =C\\cdot 1 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186497400 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 2} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{8} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 2} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{8} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186497407 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-1W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=5{e}^{2}{e}^{-x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-1W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=5$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ 5 & =C{e}^{-2} \\\\ C & =5{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=5{e}^{2}{e}^{-x}$ and $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186652941 :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(5)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(5) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 5} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{10} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" "The separated calculation below treats $W\\ne0$. The solution $W\\equiv0$ must also be included; allowing $C=0$ in the final formula supplies it.\n\nAbel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=2W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =2W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =2\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =2x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{2x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(5)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(5) & =C{e}^{2\\cdot 5} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{10} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186125812 :tutorial/content "If we know one solution to a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, we can use the **reduction of order method** to find a second solution to the equation. \n\nIt works like this: if we know that some function $y_1(t)$ is a solution, then we let $y_2(t) = u(t) y_1(t)$ for some unknown function $u(t),$ and we write down a differential equation in terms of $u.$ Solving this equation, and then computing $y_2,$ gives us our second solution.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given that $y_1(t) = \\sin{4t}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's use the reduction of order method to find a second solution $y_2(t).$\n\nAccording to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)\\sin{4t}$ for some unknown function $u(t).$ Then, we write the differential equation in terms of $u.$ To do that, we first differentiate with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u\\operatorname{sin}4t \\\\ {y}_{2}^{'} & ={u}^{'}\\operatorname{sin}4t+4u\\operatorname{cos}4t \\\\ {y}_{2}^{''} & ={u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t-16u\\operatorname{sin}4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t-16u\\operatorname{sin}4t)+16u\\operatorname{sin}4t & =0 \\\\ {u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t & =0 \\\\ {u}^{''} & =-8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cot}4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' = -8w\\cot{4t}.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 \\csc^2{4t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1 \\csc^2{4t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = -\\dfrac{c_1}{4}\\cot{4t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u \\sin{4t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = \\sin{4t}\\left( -\\dfrac{c_1}{4}\\cot{4t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = -4$ and $c_2 = 0,$ then we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = \\sin{4t}\\left( \\cot{4t} \\right) = \\cos{4t}.\n$$\n\nWhen selecting our constants $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we should try to get a solution that is \"different\" from the first. We will define precisely what we mean by \"different\" soon, but loosely speaking we should aim for a $y_2(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- None of the terms is a constant multiple of the first solution (here, we accomplished this by choosing $c_2=0$).\n\n- The remaining terms are as simple as possible (here, we accomplished this by choosing $c_1=-4$)." "If we know one solution to a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, we can use the **reduction of order method** to find a second solution to the equation. \n\nWork on an interval where the leading coefficient and the known solution are nonzero. It works like this: if we know that some function $y_1(t)$ is a solution, then we let $y_2(t) = u(t) y_1(t)$ for some unknown function $u(t),$ and we write down a differential equation in terms of $u.$ Solving this equation, and then computing $y_2,$ gives us our second solution.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given that $y_1(t) = \\sin{4t}$ is a solution to the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's use the reduction of order method to find a second solution $y_2(t).$\n\nAccording to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)\\sin{4t}$ for some unknown function $u(t).$ Then, we write the differential equation in terms of $u.$ To do that, we first differentiate with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u\\operatorname{sin}4t \\\\ {y}_{2}^{'} & ={u}^{'}\\operatorname{sin}4t+4u\\operatorname{cos}4t \\\\ {y}_{2}^{''} & ={u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t-16u\\operatorname{sin}4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t-16u\\operatorname{sin}4t)+16u\\operatorname{sin}4t & =0 \\\\ {u}^{''}\\operatorname{sin}4t+8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cos}4t & =0 \\\\ {u}^{''} & =-8{u}^{'}\\operatorname{cot}4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' = -8w\\cot{4t}.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 \\csc^2{4t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1 \\csc^2{4t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = -\\dfrac{c_1}{4}\\cot{4t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u \\sin{4t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = \\sin{4t}\\left( -\\dfrac{c_1}{4}\\cot{4t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nTo obtain a linearly independent second solution, choose $c_1\\ne0$. The choice $c_1=0$ only reproduces a multiple of the known solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = -4$ and $c_2 = 0,$ then we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = \\sin{4t}\\left( \\cot{4t} \\right) = \\cos{4t}.\n$$\n\nWhen selecting our constants $c_1$ and $c_2,$ we should try to get a solution that is \"different\" from the first. We will define precisely what we mean by \"different\" soon, but loosely speaking we should aim for a $y_2(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- None of the terms is a constant multiple of the first solution (here, we accomplished this by choosing $c_2=0$).\n\n- The remaining terms are as simple as possible (here, we accomplished this by choosing $c_1=-4$).\n\nThe divisions by $\\sin4t$ and the formula for $u$ are valid between consecutive zeros of $\\sin4t$. The simplified result $y_2=\\cos4t$ is defined on $\\mathbb R$ and directly satisfies the original equation there, so it extends across those zeros."] [:db/cas 17592186230226 :tutorial/content "Let's find the complementary solution to the following inhomogeneous ODE:\n$$\ny' + 10y = x - 8\n$$\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous ODE.\nWe start by setting the right-hand side equal to zero:\n$$\ny'_c + 10y_c = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_c}{\\textrm{d}x} = -10y_c.\n$$\n\nWe separate the variables as follows:\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}  \\frac{\\text{d} {y}_{c}}{\\text{d} x} = - 10\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\int  \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}  \\frac{\\text{d} {y}_{c}}{\\text{d} x}  \\text{d} x &  = \\int  - 10  \\text{d} x \\\\ \\int  \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}  \\text{d} {y}_{c} &  = \\int  - 10  \\text{d} x \\\\ \\ln   | {y}_{c} | &  = - 10 x + C\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we make $y_c$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle | {y}_{c} | & \\displaystyle  = {e}^{- 10 x + C} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {e}^{- 10 x} {e}^{C} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = K {e}^{- 10 x} ,\\end{array}\n$$\nwhere $K=e^{C}.$\n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the constant to be positive or negative (absorbing the sign into the constant).\nTherefore, the complementary solution is\n$$\ny_c(x)=Ae^{-10x},\n$$\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "Let's find the complementary solution to the following inhomogeneous ODE:\n$$\ny' + 10y = x - 8\n$$\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous ODE.\nWe start by setting the right-hand side equal to zero:\n$$\ny'_c + 10y_c = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_c}{\\textrm{d}x} = -10y_c.\n$$\n\nWe separate the variables as follows:\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}  \\frac{\\text{d} {y}_{c}}{\\text{d} x} = - 10\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\int  \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}  \\frac{\\text{d} {y}_{c}}{\\text{d} x}  \\text{d} x &  = \\int  - 10  \\text{d} x \\\\ \\int  \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}  \\text{d} {y}_{c} &  = \\int  - 10  \\text{d} x \\\\ \\ln   | {y}_{c} | &  = - 10 x + C\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNext, we make $y_c$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle | {y}_{c} | & \\displaystyle  = {e}^{- 10 x + C} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {e}^{- 10 x} {e}^{C} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = K {e}^{- 10 x} ,\\end{array}\n$$\nwhere $K=e^{C}.$\n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the constant to be positive or negative (absorbing the sign into the constant).\nTherefore, the complementary solution is\n$$\ny_c(x)=Ae^{-10x},\n$$\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nSeparating variables divided by $y_c$, so the zero solution must be restored explicitly. Taking $A=0$ includes it in $y_c=Ae^{-10x}$."] [:db/cas 17592186230179 :tutorial/content "We've discussed how to find the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ of a first-order inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x).\n$$\n\nWe will now turn our attention to finding a **particular solution.**\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ of a linear ODE captures the part of the solution that arises directly from the forcing term $f(x).$\nIn other words, if we substitute $y_p(x)$ into the left-hand side of the equation, it should produce the forcing function $f(x).$\n\nFor equations with constant coefficients, we can often find $y_p(x)$ by looking at the type of function appearing on the right-hand side and then guessing a form that matches it. This is sometimes called **the method of undetermined coefficients.**\n\nFor example, if the forcing function $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $n,$ we guess a particular solution that is also a polynomial of degree $n.$  After making this guess, we substitute it into the differential equation and solve for the unknown constants.\n\nNext, we'll work through a concrete example where $f(x)$ is a linear polynomial." "We've discussed how to find the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ of a first-order inhomogeneous ODE\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)= f(x).\n$$\n\nWe will now turn our attention to finding a **particular solution.**\n\nThe particular solution $y_p(x)$ of a linear ODE captures the part of the solution that arises directly from the forcing term $f(x).$\nIn other words, if we substitute $y_p(x)$ into the left-hand side of the equation, it should produce the forcing function $f(x).$\n\nFor equations with constant coefficients, we can often find $y_p(x)$ by looking at the type of function appearing on the right-hand side and then guessing a form that matches it. This is sometimes called **the method of undetermined coefficients.**\n\nFor a first-order equation with constant coefficients $ay'+by=f(x)$ and $b\ne0$, if $f(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $n$, try a general polynomial of degree $n$. If $b=0$, integrate $ay'=f(x)$ instead; the trial generally needs degree $n+1$.  After making this guess, we substitute it into the differential equation and solve for the unknown constants.\n\nNext, we'll work through a concrete example where $f(x)$ is a linear polynomial."] [:db/cas 17592186230238 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16.\n$$\n\nWe will find its general solution by computing the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n3 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using separation of variables gives\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-2/3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we will find a particular solution. To find a particular solution of\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16,\n$$\n\nnote that the forcing term is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nTherefore, we assume the particular solution is also a constant:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\beta.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y_p$ and its derivative into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 0 + 2\\beta = 16.\n$$\n\nSolving yields $\\beta = 8,$ so\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-2/3x} + 8.\n$$" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16.\n$$\n\nWe will find its general solution by computing the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n3 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using separation of variables gives\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^{-(2/3)x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we will find a particular solution. To find a particular solution of\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = 16,\n$$\n\nnote that the forcing term is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nTherefore, we assume the particular solution is also a constant:\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\beta.\n$$\n\nIts derivative is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y_p$ and its derivative into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 0 + 2\\beta = 16.\n$$\n\nSolving yields $\\beta = 8,$ so\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 8.\n$$\n\nFinally, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x) = Ae^{-(2/3)x} + 8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186230199 :tutorial/content "Consider the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\na(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove that the sum of the complementary and particular solutions gives a general solution to the equation. \n\nFirst, we note that, by definition, the complementary solution $y_c$ satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_c + b(x)y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nThis can be solved by separating the variables, and the solution is given by (for $a(x)\\neq 0$)\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\exp\\left(-\\int \\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}\\,\\textrm d x\\right).\n$$\n\nBy definition, the particular solution satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe claim that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the most general solution to the equation. \n\n- First, we show that $y_c + y_p$ satisfies the equation. Substituting this into the left-hand side of the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y & =a(x)({y}_{c}^{'}+{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)({y}_{c}+{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{[a(x){y}_{c}^{'}+b(x){y}_{c}]}_{0}+[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}] \\\\  & =a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, suppose that $y$ is *any* solution to the equation, and $y_p$ is a known, particular solution. Substituting $y - y_p$ into the left-hand side of the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x)(y-{y}_{p})^{'}+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) & =a(x)({y}^{'}-{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =[a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y]-[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is a solution, we have $a(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x)$, and since $y_p$ is a particular solution, we have $a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\left[a(x)y' + b(x)y\\right] - \\left[a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p\\right]\n= f(x) - f(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, $y - y_p$ satisfies the homogeneous equation, and hence $y - y_p = y_c$. This implies that any solution $y$ is the sum of $y_c$ and $y_p.$" "Work on an interval where $a(x)\ne0$ and the normalized coefficients $b/a$ and $f/a$ are continuous.\n\nConsider the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\na(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe'll now prove that the sum of the complementary and particular solutions gives a general solution to the equation. \n\nFirst, we note that, by definition, the complementary solution $y_c$ satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_c + b(x)y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nThis can be solved by separating the variables, and the solution is given by (for $a(x)\\neq 0$)\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\exp\\left(-\\int \\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}\\,\\textrm d x\\right).\n$$\n\nBy definition, the particular solution satisfies\n\n$$\na(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x).\n$$\n\nWe claim that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the most general solution to the equation. \n\n- First, we show that $y_c + y_p$ satisfies the equation. Substituting this into the left-hand side of the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y & =a(x)({y}_{c}^{'}+{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)({y}_{c}+{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{[a(x){y}_{c}^{'}+b(x){y}_{c}]}_{0}+[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}] \\\\  & =a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, suppose that $y$ is *any* solution to the equation, and $y_p$ is a known, particular solution. Substituting $y - y_p$ into the left-hand side of the equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x)(y-{y}_{p})^{'}+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) & =a(x)({y}^{'}-{y}_{p}^{'})+b(x)(y-{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =[a(x){y}^{'}+b(x)y]-[a(x){y}_{p}^{'}+b(x){y}_{p}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is a solution, we have $a(x)y' + b(x)y = f(x)$, and since $y_p$ is a particular solution, we have $a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p = f(x)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\left[a(x)y' + b(x)y\\right] - \\left[a(x)y'_p + b(x)y_p\\right]\n= f(x) - f(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, $y - y_p$ satisfies the homogeneous equation, and hence $y - y_p = y_c$. This implies that any solution $y$ is the sum of $y_c$ and $y_p.$"] [:db/cas 17592186153364 :tutorial/content "Recall that a second-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the equation is *homogeneous* if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x{:}$\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y =0\n$$\n\nThe **superposition principle** states that if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a linear homogeneous differential equation, then the function \n\n$$\ny(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following homogeneous differential equation:\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTwo solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x.\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that these are solutions by substituting them into the equation and verifying that they result in true statements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{''}+16{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ (-4\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {y}_{2}^{''}+16{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}4x+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the equation is linear and homogeneous, the superposition principle guarantees that the sum\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the differential equation. \n\nWe can check that this is a solution to the equation by substituting (keeping the order consistent with our definition):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{''}+16(\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x) & =0 \\\\ (-4\\operatorname{sin}4x+4\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{'}+16(\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x) & =0 \\\\ (-16\\operatorname{cos}4x-16\\operatorname{sin}4x)+16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ 0=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The superposition principle does *not* extend to the products or quotients of the known solutions. For example, the functions\n\n$$\n\\cos4x\\sin4x, \\quad \\sin^24x, \\quad \\dfrac{\\sin4x}{\\cos 4x},\n$$\n\nare *not* solutions to our original differential equation." "Recall that a second-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that the equation is *homogeneous* if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x{:}$\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y =0\n$$\n\nAll solutions and their linear combinations are considered on a common interval where they are defined.\n\nThe **superposition principle** states that if the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are solutions to a linear homogeneous differential equation, then the function \n\n$$\ny(x) = y_1(x) + y_2(x)\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the equation.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following homogeneous differential equation:\n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = 0\n$$\n\n \n\nTwo solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 4x, \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 4x.\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that these are solutions by substituting them into the equation and verifying that they result in true statements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{''}+16{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ (-4\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{cos}4x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {y}_{2}^{''}+16{y}_{2} & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{''}+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ (4\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{'}+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}4x+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the equation is linear and homogeneous, the superposition principle guarantees that the sum\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis also a solution to the differential equation. \n\nWe can check that this is a solution to the equation by substituting (keeping the order consistent with our definition):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+16y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x)^{''}+16(\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x) & =0 \\\\ (-4\\operatorname{sin}4x+4\\operatorname{cos}4x)^{'}+16(\\operatorname{cos}4x+\\operatorname{sin}4x) & =0 \\\\ (-16\\operatorname{cos}4x-16\\operatorname{sin}4x)+16\\operatorname{cos}4x+16\\operatorname{sin}4x & =0 \\\\ 0=0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The superposition principle does *not* extend to the products or quotients of the known solutions. For example, the functions\n\n$$\n\\cos4x\\sin4x, \\quad \\sin^24x, \\quad \\dfrac{\\sin4x}{\\cos 4x},\n$$\n\nare *not* solutions to our original differential equation."] [:db/cas 17592186153328 :tutorial/content "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n$$\nW(y_1,y_2) = \t\n\t\t{\\left| \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } & {y}_{2}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right|}\t\n= y_1y_2'- y_2 y_1',\n$$\nand it has the following important properties:\n\nIf $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\nIf $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot deduce any information from $W = 0$ in cases where $y_1$ and $y_2$ are not known to be solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation." "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n$$\nW(y_1,y_2) = \t\n\t\t{\\left| \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } & {y}_{2}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right|}\t\n= y_1y_2'- y_2 y_1',\n$$\nand it has the following important properties:\n\nIf $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\nIf $y_1$ and $y_2$ solve the same homogeneous equation $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$ with $p$ and $q$ continuous on $I$, and $W(x_0)=0$ at one point $x_0\\in I$, then they are linearly dependent on $I$. The continuity and common-interval assumptions are essential.\n\n**Watch out!** For arbitrary differentiable functions, an identically zero Wronskian alone does not establish linear dependence. Extra hypotheses are needed."] [:db/cas 17592186153329 :tutorial/content "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{2x})}^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions." "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of order $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{2x})}^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of order two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186153382 :tutorial/content "Consider the second-order linear homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 5y' + 6y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that a fundamental set of solutions is given by\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle tells us that any linear combination of solutions to a linear, homogeneous differential equation is also a solution to the equation. Therefore, the **general solution** to this differential equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are **arbitrary constants.**\n\nIn general, if an $n$th order homogeneous, linear ODE has a fundamental set of solutions given by \n\n$$\ny_1(x), \\quad y_2(x),\\quad \\ldots, \\quad y_n(x),\n$$\n\nthen the general solution to the equation is \n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = C_1y_1(x)+C_2y_2(x)+\\cdots+C_ny_n(x)}\n$$\n\nfor arbitrary constants $C_1, C_2,\\ldots C_n.$\n\n**Note:** Using sigma notation, we can write the general solution more compactly as\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n C_i y_i(x).\n$$" "Work throughout on one interval where the leading coefficient is nonzero and all normalized coefficients are continuous.\n\nConsider the second-order linear homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 5y' + 6y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that a fundamental set of solutions is given by\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle tells us that any linear combination of solutions to a linear, homogeneous differential equation is also a solution to the equation. Therefore, the **general solution** to this differential equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are **arbitrary constants.**\n\nIn general, if an $n$th order homogeneous, linear ODE has a fundamental set of solutions given by \n\n$$\ny_1(x), \\quad y_2(x),\\quad \\ldots, \\quad y_n(x),\n$$\n\nthen the general solution to the equation is \n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = C_1y_1(x)+C_2y_2(x)+\\cdots+C_ny_n(x)}\n$$\n\nfor arbitrary constants $C_1, C_2,\\ldots C_n.$\n\n**Note:** Using sigma notation, we can write the general solution more compactly as\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n C_i y_i(x).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186153335 :tutorial/content "Similar to first-order linear ODEs, for inhomogeneous differential equations, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the **complementary solution** (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a **particular solution** of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWe will apply this structure to a specific differential equation in the next slide." "Work throughout on one interval where the leading coefficient is nonzero and all normalized coefficients are continuous.\n\nSimilar to first-order linear ODEs, for inhomogeneous differential equations, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the **complementary solution** (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a **particular solution** of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWe will apply this structure to a specific differential equation in the next slide."] [:db/cas 17592186218358 :tutorial/content "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on some interval $I.$ Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n$$\nW(x)=\n{\\left| \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1} ( x ) & {y}_{2} ( x ) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( x ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( x )\\end{array} \\right|}\n=\ny_1(x)y_2'(x)-y_2(x)y_1'(x).\n$$\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation via separation of variables gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity." "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on an interval $I$ where $P$ and $Q$ are continuous. Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n$$\nW(x)=\n{\\left| \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1} ( x ) & {y}_{2} ( x ) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } ( x ) & {y}_{2}^{\\prime } ( x )\\end{array} \\right|}\n=\ny_1(x)y_2'(x)-y_2(x)y_1'(x).\n$$\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation with an integrating factor (which includes $W\\equiv0$) gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity."] [:db/cas 17592186218359 :tutorial/content "*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{′}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{′}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required." "Assume $p$ and $q$ are continuous on a common interval $I$, and both solutions are defined there.\n\n*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{′}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{′}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required."] [:db/cas 17592186218360 :tutorial/content "Because the exponential factor in Abel's identity is *never zero*, we can deduce the following facts:\n\n- If $W(x_0)=0$ at some point $x_0 \\in I,$ then $W(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\n- If $W(x_0)\\neq 0,$ then $W(x) \\neq 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\nLet's see how this works on concrete examples." "Throughout this argument, the normalized coefficients are continuous on one common interval $I$.\n\nBecause the exponential factor in Abel's identity is *never zero*, we can deduce the following facts:\n\n- If $W(x_0)=0$ at some point $x_0 \\in I,$ then $W(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\n- If $W(x_0)\\neq 0,$ then $W(x) \\neq 0$ for all $x \\in I.$\n\nLet's see how this works on concrete examples."] [:db/cas 17592186314513 :answer-field/correct 17592186313864 17592186313471] [:db/cas 17592186314549 :answer-field/correct 17592186311983 17592186313348] [:db/cas 17592186314590 :answer-field/correct 17592186313017 17592186312476] [:db/cas 17592186314054 :answer-field/correct 17592186312739 17592186312511] [:db/cas 17592186314179 :answer-field/correct 17592186313919 17592186311990] [:db/cas 17592186314586 :answer-field/correct 17592186312911 17592186313954] [:db/cas 17592186314145 :answer-field/correct 17592186313097 17592186312693] [:db/cas 17592186314250 :answer-field/correct 17592186312914 17592186312469] [:db/cas 17592186314572 :answer-field/correct 17592186313623 17592186312988] {:db/id "answer-quiz3-q332773" :answer/id "912bc8b8-7a5a-5b2a-b46b-bf9e6d8ede5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4e^{-3x}+2"} [:db/add 17592186228106 :answer-field/choices "answer-quiz3-q332773"] [:db/retract 17592186152642 :question/answer-fields 17592186150359] [:db/retract 17592186217803 :question/answer-fields 17592186216678] [:db/retract 17592186317015 :answer-field/choices 17592186316190] [:db/retract 17592186316650 :answer-field/choices 17592186315430] [:db/retract 17592186314513 :answer-field/choices 17592186313864] [:db/retract 17592186314549 :answer-field/choices 17592186311983] [:db/retract 17592186314590 :answer-field/choices 17592186313017] [:db/retract 17592186314054 :answer-field/choices 17592186312739] [:db/retract 17592186314179 :answer-field/choices 17592186313919] [:db/retract 17592186314586 :answer-field/choices 17592186312911] [:db/retract 17592186314145 :answer-field/choices 17592186313097] [:db/retract 17592186314250 :answer-field/choices 17592186312914] [:db/retract 17592186314572 :answer-field/choices 17592186313623] [:db/retract 17592186316964 :answer-field/choices 17592186316497] [:db/retract 17592186317078 :answer-field/choices 17592186316341] [:db/ensure "answer-quiz3-q332773" :answer/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186125180 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186335772 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186124369 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 1759